osx-gpu: glQueryCounter flushes immediate-mode vertices first

glBeginQuery and glEndQuery flush what glBegin/glEnd has buffered before
they act (FLUSH_VERTICES); glQueryCounter did not, so a GL_TIMESTAMP query
was stamped before the draws issued ahead of it, and two glQueryCounter
calls around immediate-mode drawing bracketed nothing. Found with
tools/guest/begl33test (bridge, "D1 timer query GL_TIMESTAMP": a stamp, 100
quads, a stamp) on Vega 20, 2026-11-15: its command processor runs the two
bottom-of-pipe events inside one step of the GPU's counter (160 ns), so the
stamps came out equal in about half the runs; slower cards had always
separated them by the events' own cost and passed.

diff --git a/src/mesa/main/queryobj.c b/src/mesa/main/queryobj.c
--- a/src/mesa/main/queryobj.c
+++ b/src/mesa/main/queryobj.c
@@ -945,6 +945,11 @@
    q->Ready = GL_FALSE;
    q->EverBound = GL_TRUE;
 
+   /* osx-gpu: the timestamp is taken where the command stands in the stream:
+    * after what was drawn before it, immediate-mode vertices included.
+    */
+   FLUSH_VERTICES(ctx, 0, 0);
+
    /* QueryCounter is implemented using EndQuery without BeginQuery
     * in drivers. This is actually Direct3D and Gallium convention.
     */
